k drum with a missing cap that had taken on water. Those 11 vans are exactly the 11 vans with soft-pedal complaints. The two vans that got fluid from a new drum have no complaints. Two vans retested on 24 September had fluid boiling at about 150°C, against 255°C for fresh fluid. Fluid that takes on water can vaporise on long descents, which makes the pedal go soft. The same drivers ran Upper Wend in other vans, 141 days in all, with no brake reports. Tessaro pads were fitted at both garages and in-house, so they don't explain it.\n\nWhat to do this week: pull the 11 vans from Northfield's bulk drum (and, to be safe, all 13 Northfield-serviced vans) off the road. Test and renew their fluid from sealed stock, then return them. Do not discipline drivers or start retraining. Also check what the other garage, Calder Road, used. I'm fairly confident, about 85–90%. The squeal link to the Tessaro pads is still unresolved, so hold off on changing pad supplier.\n","numLines":21,"startLine":1,"totalLines":21}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01CshVyEyRw8eYuJYoFrKhSd","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
